---
description: Require actionable checklists in project Markdown plans
alwaysApply: true
---

# Planning documents

- When creating or substantially updating an implementation plan or project
  Markdown document, include actionable Markdown checkboxes (`- [ ]`).
- Use unchecked boxes for work that is not verified and checked boxes only for
  work completed with evidence.
- A checked box's own sentence must stay true. Do not check a claim that the
  following note negates.
- When a gate defers implementation, leave those task boxes unchecked and keep
  their original sentences. Record the deferral in the linked evidence file and
  a short note under the step. Do not rewrite the tasks into checked deferrals.
- An unrun conditional feature is **not authorized**, not **pass** or **fail**.
  Use **fail** only after the pre-registered pass rule was scored.
- Keep every item under a `## Learning objectives` section unchecked. Those
  lists are study topics, not implementation or verification checklists.
- For verification checkboxes, record or link the command and result when the
  evidence is not obvious from the changed files.
- Before running or recording a verification command, confirm every referenced
  file, test path, marker, and CLI option exists in the current checkout.
- Do not mark full-suite, integration, or lint checks complete when they were
  not run successfully; distinguish unavailable tooling from a clean result.
- Recheck broad verification claims after later milestones change the codebase,
  and uncheck or annotate them when newer evidence invalidates the claim.
- Keep the checklist close to the relevant steps so it can be updated as work
  progresses.
- Do not commit or push planning-document changes without explicit user
  approval.
- When closing a workstream milestone, sync the plan **Status**, **Execution
  sequence**, **Acceptance criteria**, and linked **evidence** file so unchecked
  boxes do not contradict implemented code or recorded deferrals.
- When marking an implementation **step** complete in a workstream plan, update
  that step’s checkboxes and evidence in the same pass, and refresh **Current
  baseline** (especially **Open baseline gaps** and the canonical module table)
  so the plan does not still describe the pre-step codebase. Do not rely on a
  later review to discover drift.
- When a plan mixes **documentation authoring** with a later **user-gated** step
  (e.g. “OK to start Phase N code”), split **Goal / Exit** and **Verification**
  into two labeled gates. Mark authoring **closed** only after deliverable docs
  exist; leave explicit user-approval checkboxes unchecked until the user
  approves in writing. Do not use a single “Phase complete” verdict that blurs
  the two.
- On close, align **Status**, parent transition-doc section numbers (e.g. § 2.4
  exit criteria), and **Acceptance criteria** so section references and status
  strings (`draft`, `planning-complete`, `closed (authoring)`) match the linked
  child plan.
